course: dictionary
lesson: 400
title: Appeal: many meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and context tells you which one you need. «Police appeal for witnesses» means the police ask strongly for people who saw something to come forward. «It's the jolly times on shore that appeal to me» means those times are attractive and you like them.
explain: Appeal can also be a noun: «a school appeal for the food bank» is a request, often for money or food. In cricket, players appeal to the umpire by shouting «Howzat!». In law, a person can appeal to a higher court to change a decision.
explain: Grown-ups also use appeal in arguments. «We've always done it this way» is an appeal to tradition: it relies on how things have always been done, not on whether they work. Check the reason behind the words, not just how attractive they sound.
fact: To appeal for something means to ask for it strongly, often from the public.
fact: If something appeals to you, you like it or find it attractive.
fact: In law, to appeal means to ask a higher court or person to change a decision.
fact: An appeal to tradition means arguing that something is right only because it has always been done.
fact: Some online appeals are scams; real charities are registered and can be checked on an official charity register.
exercise: The police «appeal for witnesses» after the break-in. What does appeal mean here? | Change a decision in court | Shout at the umpire | Like something very much | *Ask strongly for help
exercise: «It's the jolly times on shore that appeal to me.» What does appeals to me mean? | I am shouting about them | I am taking them to court | *I find them attractive and I like them | I must ask someone for them
exercise: «We've always done it this way» is an appeal to tradition. Why is it not a reason on its own? | Tradition is a kind of sport | Old ways are always wrong | *Old ways can be good or bad, so you must check whether they work | Old ways are always right
test: In cricket, players appeal to whom? | The class teacher | *The umpire | The shopkeeper | The bus driver
test: What is an appeal for a school charity drive? | A court case about a bad decision | *A request, often for money or food, for a good cause | A rude reply to a friend | A type of cricket shot
exam: Which is an example of an appeal to the expert who isn't? | Checking a doctor's advice before you follow it | *Trusting a footballer's advice on healthy food just because he is brilliant at football | Asking your teacher to explain a tricky sum | Reading the label on a tin of soup
